    "lineStart": 0,
    "memoText": "\n \n                         VETO MESSAGE - No. 224\n \nTO THE SENATE:\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n \nSenate Bill Number 5430, entitled:\n \n    \"AN  ACT to amend the public service law, in relation to the minimum\n      requirements for a quorum\"\n \n    NOT APPROVED\n \n  This bill would amend the Public Service Law (PSL) which mandates that\na majority of the total number of members of the Public Service  Commis-\nsion  (PSC) are required to constitute a quorum and that no fewer than a\nmajority of the total number of statutory members must vote affirmative-\nly to transact business.\n \n  Existing law, provides that the PSC shall consist  of  at  least  five\nmembers,  and that a majority of the commissioners in attendance consti-\ntutes a quorum. The proposed legislation seeks  to  clarify  that  there\nmust  be at least three PSC commissioners present in order to constitute\na quorum and that, at a  minimum,  all  three  commissioners  must  vote\naffirmatively  to take action. Identical bills were vetoed in 2017 (Veto\nNo. 210) and 2018 (Veto No. 340) on the grounds  that  such  bill  would\nseverely  restrict  the  ability  of  the  PSC  to conduct business. The\nCommission is faced with issues of public safety and  welfare,  such  as\nthose  presented  by utility system emergencies. Therefore, the PSC must\nbe empowered to act with a majority of appointed commissioners present.\n \n  As stated in previous veto memoranda, the PSC  also  has  a  statutory\nresponsibility  to  respond  in rate proceedings in a specific amount of\ntime, otherwise, such increases would take effect by operation  of  law.\nIt would be untenable to allow ratepayers to suffer such increases mere-\nly  because of vacancies or absences of PSC members. In situations where\na full panel of Commissioners is not present or has not been  appointed,\nthe  PSC  must  be  able to rely on the expertise of those Commissioners\npresent. The Legislature saw fit to amend the PSL in 1954 to  adopt  the\nmajority  quorum requirement, in recognition that members of the Commis-\nsion could be absent. Because enactment  of  this  bill  would  put  the\npublic and rate payers at risk, I am constrained to veto this bill.\n \n  The bill is disapproved.                    (signed) ANDREW M. CUOMO\n \n                               __________\n",
